WEBVTT

00:00:00.362 --> 00:00:02.371
Imagine a launcher on top of a mountain

00:00:02.632 --> 00:00:04.802
so tall it pokes right out of the

00:00:04.902 --> 00:00:04.983
air.

00:00:05.726 --> 00:00:08.338
Fire a ball sideways, and it curves down

00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:09.745
and hits the ground a little way off.

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:12.868
Fire it faster, and it lands further round

00:00:12.889 --> 00:00:13.311
the planet.

00:00:14.134 --> 00:00:16.576
Newton asked this over three hundred years ago:

00:00:17.196 --> 00:00:19.398
what happens if you keep firing it faster

00:00:19.458 --> 00:00:19.997
and faster?

00:00:21.047 --> 00:00:21.413
Have a think.

00:00:22.335 --> 00:00:22.843
Here's the trick.

00:00:23.548 --> 00:00:26.534
In its first second, any projectile falls about

00:00:26.635 --> 00:00:27.717
four point nine metres.

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:30.907
And Earth's surface curves away below a straight

00:00:30.928 --> 00:00:33.475
line by about five metres over the first

00:00:33.656 --> 00:00:34.819
eight kilometres you travel.

00:00:35.542 --> 00:00:38.464
So a ball launched sideways at about eight

00:00:38.525 --> 00:00:41.628
kilometres per second falls five metres in that

00:00:41.769 --> 00:00:42.429
first second,

00:00:42.991 --> 00:00:45.112
and the ground drops away five metres too.

00:00:45.914 --> 00:00:47.957
By then its direction has turned a little,

00:00:48.318 --> 00:00:50.481
so the next second starts along a new

00:00:50.522 --> 00:00:52.784
straight line, and the same thing happens again.

00:00:53.509 --> 00:00:55.443
It keeps falling, and it keeps missing.

00:00:56.068 --> 00:00:56.608
That's an orbit,

00:00:57.111 --> 00:00:59.316
falling around Earth, not into it.

00:01:00.189 --> 00:01:02.297
Once it's moving, the only force on it

00:01:02.438 --> 00:01:04.486
is gravity, pointing to Earth's centre.

00:01:05.169 --> 00:01:07.454
In a circular orbit, that force is always

00:01:07.515 --> 00:01:08.858
at right angles to the velocity.

00:01:09.621 --> 00:01:12.086
As we saw with circular motion, a sideways

00:01:12.147 --> 00:01:15.033
force like that changes the velocity's direction but

00:01:15.155 --> 00:01:17.480
not its speed, and it does no work.

00:01:18.082 --> 00:01:20.739
So gravity is the inward resultant force.

00:01:21.363 --> 00:01:23.868
Centripetal force isn't an extra force here, it's

00:01:23.909 --> 00:01:25.412
the job gravity is doing.

00:01:25.995 --> 00:01:28.087
So a satellite doesn't need its engines running

00:01:28.249 --> 00:01:28.731
to stay up.

00:01:29.488 --> 00:01:31.234
That gives us the orbital speed.

00:01:31.978 --> 00:01:34.447
For a circle of radius r, the inward

00:01:34.488 --> 00:01:37.177
force needed is m v squared over r.

00:01:37.842 --> 00:01:40.654
Gravity supplies big G M m over r

00:01:40.715 --> 00:01:41.116
squared.

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:43.937
In a stable circular orbit, they're equal.

00:01:44.622 --> 00:01:47.314
The satellite's mass m is on both sides,

00:01:47.556 --> 00:01:48.219
so it cancels.

00:01:48.982 --> 00:01:51.907
Multiply both sides by r, and v squared

00:01:52.108 --> 00:01:54.152
equals big G M over r.

00:01:54.774 --> 00:01:56.881
So v equals the square root of big

00:01:56.902 --> 00:01:59.489
G M over r, with r measured from

00:01:59.510 --> 00:02:00.232
Earth's centre.

00:02:01.016 --> 00:02:04.008
Because m cancelled, the orbital speed doesn't depend

00:02:04.069 --> 00:02:05.032
on the satellite's mass.

00:02:05.696 --> 00:02:07.599
A space station and a spanner at the

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:10.143
same radius need exactly the same speed,

00:02:10.765 --> 00:02:13.129
and they have the same acceleration towards Earth.

00:02:13.871 --> 00:02:16.399
That's why loose objects float inside a station.

00:02:16.962 --> 00:02:19.367
The station and everything in it fall together,

00:02:19.749 --> 00:02:21.353
so nothing presses on the floor.

00:02:21.975 --> 00:02:24.203
They aren't free of gravity, they're in free

00:02:24.264 --> 00:02:24.584
fall.

00:02:25.568 --> 00:02:27.492
Let's find the speed of a satellite four

00:02:27.533 --> 00:02:29.978
hundred kilometres up, about where the space station

00:02:30.059 --> 00:02:30.359
orbits.

00:02:31.182 --> 00:02:33.605
First, r from the centre: six point three

00:02:33.666 --> 00:02:36.389
seven one plus zero point four zero zero

00:02:36.731 --> 00:02:39.174
gives six point seven seven one times ten

00:02:39.195 --> 00:02:40.015
to the six metres.

00:02:40.637 --> 00:02:42.681
Then v is the square root of four

00:02:42.722 --> 00:02:44.766
point zero zero two times ten to the

00:02:44.807 --> 00:02:46.430
fourteen, over that radius.

00:02:46.953 --> 00:02:49.196
That's about seven point six nine times ten

00:02:49.237 --> 00:02:51.701
to the three metres per second, nearly seven

00:02:51.742 --> 00:02:53.525
point seven kilometres every second.

00:02:54.302 --> 00:02:55.890
Notice r is on the bottom.

00:02:56.453 --> 00:02:58.681
A higher orbit has a weaker pull, so

00:02:58.721 --> 00:03:00.086
it needs less speed to match it.

00:03:00.869 --> 00:03:03.120
Four times the radius, and the speed halves.

00:03:03.741 --> 00:03:06.085
That surprises a lot of people: the satellites

00:03:06.185 --> 00:03:08.167
furthest out are the slowest ones.

00:03:08.908 --> 00:03:11.097
The Moon, way out, moves at only about

00:03:11.277 --> 00:03:12.542
one kilometre per second.

00:03:13.717 --> 00:03:14.903
How long does one lap take?

00:03:15.707 --> 00:03:18.412
In one period, T, the satellite travels one

00:03:18.453 --> 00:03:19.054
circumference,

00:03:19.556 --> 00:03:22.141
so v equals two pi r over T.

00:03:22.862 --> 00:03:24.943
Put that into the force equation, and with

00:03:24.984 --> 00:03:25.904
a little rearranging, r

00:03:26.565 --> 00:03:29.247
cubed over T squared equals big G M

00:03:29.428 --> 00:03:30.568
over four pi squared.

00:03:31.149 --> 00:03:33.928
That's the circular-orbit form of Kepler's third law.

00:03:34.848 --> 00:03:37.191
For our satellite, T is two pi r

00:03:37.332 --> 00:03:37.792
over v:

00:03:38.374 --> 00:03:40.537
two pi times six point seven seven one

00:03:40.578 --> 00:03:42.821
times ten to the six, over seven thousand

00:03:42.882 --> 00:03:43.943
six hundred and eighty-eight.

00:03:44.545 --> 00:03:46.630
That's about five thousand five hundred and thirty

00:03:46.691 --> 00:03:49.919
seconds, or ninety-two minutes, around sixteen laps a

00:03:49.940 --> 00:03:50.139
day.

00:03:50.904 --> 00:03:52.813
NASA quotes about ninety minutes for the space

00:03:52.834 --> 00:03:54.542
station, so the model works well.

00:03:55.257 --> 00:03:56.670
Here's the powerful part.

00:03:57.275 --> 00:03:59.876
The right-hand side has only big G and

00:04:00.017 --> 00:04:00.616
Earth's mass,

00:04:01.178 --> 00:04:03.638
so r cubed over T squared is the

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:05.500
same for everything orbiting Earth.

00:04:06.342 --> 00:04:08.806
Try the Moon: about three hundred and eighty-four

00:04:08.907 --> 00:04:12.172
thousand kilometres away, going round once every twenty-seven

00:04:12.213 --> 00:04:12.914
point three days.

00:04:13.616 --> 00:04:16.127
Its r cubed over T squared comes out

00:04:16.188 --> 00:04:18.517
within one per cent of the satellite's value.

00:04:19.301 --> 00:04:21.085
Two orbits that look nothing alike,

00:04:21.668 --> 00:04:22.810
one shared constant.

00:04:23.877 --> 00:04:24.962
Here's the pattern to remember:

00:04:25.506 --> 00:04:27.475
gravity is the centripetal force.

00:04:28.198 --> 00:04:31.080
Write gravity equal to m v squared over

00:04:31.342 --> 00:04:33.743
r, cancel the m, and everything about a

00:04:33.824 --> 00:04:36.946
circular orbit follows, the speed, the period, and

00:04:36.987 --> 00:04:37.947
the constant ratio.

00:04:38.729 --> 00:04:40.210
And remember what an orbit is:

00:04:40.892 --> 00:04:43.114
falling around, not falling down.

00:04:45.863 --> 00:04:46.266
Your turn.

00:04:46.951 --> 00:04:49.714
A space telescope orbits five hundred and forty-seven

00:04:49.754 --> 00:04:51.796
kilometres above Earth's surface.

00:04:52.437 --> 00:04:55.701
Assuming a circular orbit, calculate its orbital speed,

00:04:56.222 --> 00:04:57.484
and its period in minutes.

00:04:58.207 --> 00:04:59.781
Use the data sheet values for Earth.

00:05:00.447 --> 00:05:01.413
Pause here to answer.

00:05:12.760 --> 00:05:15.485
First, r equals six point three seven one

00:05:15.626 --> 00:05:18.170
plus zero point five four seven, which is

00:05:18.371 --> 00:05:20.334
six point nine one eight times ten to

00:05:20.354 --> 00:05:21.196
the six metres.

00:05:21.937 --> 00:05:23.901
Then v is the square root of big

00:05:23.941 --> 00:05:26.647
G M over r, about seven point six

00:05:26.767 --> 00:05:28.531
one times ten to the three metres per

00:05:28.571 --> 00:05:28.871
second.

00:05:29.614 --> 00:05:32.319
T is two pi r over v, about

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:35.706
five thousand seven hundred seconds, or ninety-five minutes.

00:05:36.570 --> 00:05:38.574
The classic slip is using five hundred and

00:05:38.595 --> 00:05:39.858
forty-seven kilometres as r.

00:05:41.964 --> 00:05:42.389
Last one.

00:05:43.172 --> 00:05:46.216
Satellite Y orbits Earth at four times the

00:05:46.376 --> 00:05:48.139
orbital radius of satellite X.

00:05:48.761 --> 00:05:50.090
Both orbits are circular.

00:05:50.814 --> 00:05:53.643
How do Y's orbital speed and period compare

00:05:53.684 --> 00:05:54.306
with X's?

00:05:55.110 --> 00:05:55.937
Pause here to answer.

00:06:06.574 --> 00:06:08.775
Speed goes as one over the square root

00:06:08.836 --> 00:06:08.935
of r,

00:06:09.637 --> 00:06:12.038
so four times the radius gives half the

00:06:12.079 --> 00:06:12.318
speed.

00:06:13.182 --> 00:06:13.929
Now the period.

00:06:14.853 --> 00:06:17.117
Y's lap is four times longer, and it

00:06:17.178 --> 00:06:19.343
moves at half the speed, so it takes

00:06:19.584 --> 00:06:20.706
eight times as long.

00:06:21.388 --> 00:06:23.389
The slip to avoid is saying four times

00:06:23.470 --> 00:06:25.531
as long, just because the radius is four

00:06:25.552 --> 00:06:26.052
times bigger.

00:06:27.940 --> 00:06:28.061
So

00:06:28.761 --> 00:06:31.204
gravity holds a satellite in orbit by bending

00:06:31.244 --> 00:06:31.725
its path,

00:06:32.245 --> 00:06:34.508
and the radius fixes both its speed and

00:06:34.568 --> 00:06:35.049
its period.

00:06:35.671 --> 00:06:37.744
That means if you choose the period, you've

00:06:37.784 --> 00:06:38.650
chosen the radius.

00:06:39.393 --> 00:06:41.576
Which period would let a satellite keep pace

00:06:41.616 --> 00:06:43.738
with Earth's spin, so it hangs over one

00:06:43.798 --> 00:06:44.039
spot?

00:06:44.746 --> 00:06:45.214
That's next.

00:06:47.961 --> 00:06:48.784
Here are the key notes.

00:06:49.307 --> 00:06:50.855
Pause the video if you need more time

00:06:50.895 --> 00:06:51.317
to copy them.
